WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
TensorFlow.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:05.810
Google's framework has the opposite personality from PyTorch.

3
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.533
It wants to compile your model into a graph.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.733
TensorFlow two point x offers two ways to run.

5
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.833
Eager mode runs line by line, just like PyTorch or NumPy.

6
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:22.100
Decorate a function with tf function, and TensorFlow traces it once into a graph.

7
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.367
That makes Python side effects like print fire only during tracing.

8
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.700
So graph mode is faster, but debugging gets harder.

9
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.267
Here's the life of a decorated function.

10
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.300
On the first call, TensorFlow reads your Python.

11
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.100
It traces the tensor operations and freezes them into an optimized graph.

12
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.067
Every later call reruns that graph without touching Python again.

13
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.267
Here's a training step in TensorFlow.

14
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:52.000
The decorator on top turns the whole function into a graph.

15
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.567
Unlike PyTorch, we open a gradient tape, and everything inside it gets recorded.

16
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:01.440
We ask the tape for gradients, then apply them to the weights.

17
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.033
After two hundred steps, the line is learned.

18
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:07.563
So why learn it if you write PyTorch?

19
00:01:07.563 --> 00:01:10.333
Because so much production ML is TensorFlow.

20
00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:14.933
The SavedModel format bundles the graph and weights into one directory.

21
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:19.133
TF Serving loads that directory to serve models at scale.

22
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:23.000
And TF Lite carries models onto mobile devices.

23
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.433
Picture this handoff.

24
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:29.533
A satellite team gives you their cloud detector as a SavedModel.

25
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.210
You don't rewrite it.

26
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:37.767
You load it, inspect its signature, and wrap it in the same FastAPI endpoint as your PyTorch models.

27
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:41.733
Reading TensorFlow fluently turns a sprint into an afternoon.

28
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:43.507
To recap.

29
00:01:43.507 --> 00:01:46.500
TensorFlow can compile your model into a graph.

30
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.100
Tf function traces once, then reruns that graph.

31
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.400
SavedModel is the deployment format for serving.

32
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.244
You may write PyTorch, but read TensorFlow fluently.

33
00:01:57.244 --> 00:01:58.267
Next up: Keras.
